/-!
# Split exactness of the adic completion of a short exact sequence with flat quotient

Let `R` be a commutative ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `0 → K → P → M → 0` be a short
exact sequence of `R`-modules. If `M` is flat over `R` and the reduction `M/IM` is a projective
`R ⧸ I`-module, then the sequence of `I`-adic completions

  `0 → K^∧ → P^∧ → M^∧ → 0`

is split exact: it is short exact, and `P^∧ → M^∧` admits an `R^∧`-linear section, so `M^∧`
is a direct summand of `P^∧`.

Flatness of `M` makes every `0 → K/IⁿK → P/IⁿP → M/IⁿM → 0` short exact, and short exactness
survives the passage to the limit. The section is assembled from compatible splittings
`sₙ : M/IⁿM → P/IⁿP`: the first exists because `M/IM` is projective over `R ⧸ I`, and `M/Iⁿ⁺¹M`
is again projective over `R ⧸ Iⁿ⁺¹` (Stacks 05CG), which lifts `sₙ` to `sₙ₊₁`.

## Main results

* `AdicCompletion.map_splitExact_of_projective_quotient_smul_top`: the `I`-adic completion of a
  short exact sequence whose quotient is flat with projective reduction modulo `I` is split
  exact.

## Implementation notes

The short exact sequence is given as a pair of `R`-linear maps together with
`Function.Injective f`, `Function.Exact f g` and `Function.Surjective g`, Mathlib's spelling of a
short exact sequence of modules at the level of linear maps. Mathlib carries no adic completion
functor on `ModuleCat R`, so there is no short complex in which to phrase the conclusion as
`CategoryTheory.ShortComplex.Split`; it is spelled out instead as: `K^∧ → P^∧` is injective, the
pair `(K^∧ → P^∧, P^∧ → M^∧)` is exact, and `P^∧ → M^∧` has a right inverse. Surjectivity of
`P^∧ → M^∧` is not listed, being an immediate consequence of that right inverse (and holding with
no hypothesis at all, `AdicCompletion.map_surjective`). Together the three conjuncts say that the
completed sequence is isomorphic to `0 → K^∧ → K^∧ ⊕ M^∧ → M^∧ → 0`, which is what the source
means by a split exact sequence.

The section is asked to be `AdicCompletion I R`-linear, not merely `R`-linear. This is the
stronger statement, and it is what the source produces: the splittings `sₙ` are `R ⧸ Iⁿ`-linear,
so their limit is linear over `R^∧ = lim R/Iⁿ`.

`M/IM` is `M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule R M)` with Mathlib's canonical `R ⧸ I`-action, the spelling of
`LinearMap.reduceModIdeal` and of the companion result Stacks 05CG, so the two compose without a
translation lemma. Flatness is an instance argument, being a class. No finiteness hypothesis on
the modules and no Noetherian hypothesis on `R` occur, the source needing neither; in particular
the exactness half is not an instance of `AdicCompletion.map_exact` and
`AdicCompletion.map_injective`, which assume `R` Noetherian and the modules finite, flatness of
`M` doing that work here. The three modules live in three unrelated universes, as the maps on
completions do in Mathlib.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is idle: `hf`, `hfg` and `hg` are the short exact sequence whose
completion is the conclusion, and each is used — `hf` for injectivity of `K^∧ → P^∧`, `hfg` for
exactness at `P^∧`, `hg` for the surjectivity of `P/IⁿP → M/IⁿM` that the splittings section.
Flatness of `M` is what makes `0 → K/IⁿK → P/IⁿP` exact, and dropping it breaks the conclusion:
for `R = ℤ`, `I = 2ℤ` and `0 → ℤ → ℤ → ℤ/2 → 0` with `f` multiplication by `2`, the reduction
`M/IM = ℤ/2` is projective over `ℤ/2`, and the completed sequence `0 → ℤ₂ → ℤ₂ → ℤ/2 → 0` is
short exact but not split, `ℤ₂` being torsion free. Projectivity of `M/IM` cannot be dropped
either: for `I = 0` the completions are the identity, `M = ℚ` over `R = ℤ` is flat, and no
surjection from a free module onto `ℚ` splits. (2) The scalars are the source's: the sequence and
the flatness hypothesis are over `R`, only the reduction `M/IM` is a module over `R ⧸ I`, and the
completions and the maps between them are over `R^∧`. (3) The two canonical maps of the completed
sequence are pinned to `AdicCompletion.map I f` and `AdicCompletion.map I g` rather than merely
asserted to exist, and the splitting is pinned to a right inverse of the latter. (4) A witness:
`R = ℤ`, `I = 2ℤ`, `P = ℤ × ℤ`, `g = LinearMap.fst`, `K = ℤ` and `f = LinearMap.inr`; here `I` and
all three modules are nonzero, `M = ℤ` is flat and `M/IM = ℤ/2` is free over `ℤ ⧸ 2ℤ`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 05D3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05D3)

## Tags

adic completion, split exact sequence, flat module, projective module
-/

