/-!
# Completion of an extension whose quotient is killed by a power of the ideal

Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `0 → M → N → Q → 0` be a short exact sequence
of `R`-modules with `IᶜQ = 0`. Then `I`-adic completion produces a short exact sequence
`0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q^∧ → 0`, and `Q` is already `I`-adically complete, so that the third term is the
source's `Q`.

Completeness of `Q` is immediate: the filtration `IⁿQ` is `0` for `n ≥ c`, so an `I`-adic Cauchy
sequence in `Q` is constant from index `c` on and the intersection of the filtration vanishes.

The content is the left half of the sequence, and the mechanism is that `IᶜQ = 0` makes the two
filtrations `{IⁿM}` and `{M ∩ IⁿN}` of `M` cofinal in each other. Indeed `IᶜN` maps into `IᶜQ = 0`,
so `IᶜN ⊆ f(M)`, whence

`Iⁿ⁺ᶜN = Iⁿ(IᶜN) ⊆ Iⁿf(M) = f(IⁿM)`,

which with injectivity of `f` says that `f x ∈ Iⁿ⁺ᶜN` forces `x ∈ IⁿM`. Both remaining conjuncts
are that one statement applied to a Cauchy sequence, shifted by `c`. For injectivity, if a Cauchy
sequence `a` in `M` has `f(aₙ) ∈ IⁿN` for all `n`, then `a_{n+c} ∈ IⁿM`, and a Cauchy sequence
lying in the filtration after a shift represents `0`. For exactness, if a Cauchy sequence `b` in
`N` has `g(bₙ) ∈ IⁿQ` for all `n`, then `g(b_{n+c}) = 0`, so `b_{n+c} = f(aₙ)` for a unique `aₙ`;
the displayed containment turns the Cauchy estimate `b_{p+c} ≡ b_{q+c} mod I^{p+c}N` into
`a_p ≡ a_q mod IᵖM`, so `a` is Cauchy, and `f(aₙ) = b_{n+c}` says exactly that the class of `a`
maps to the class of `b`.

Surjectivity of `N^∧ → Q^∧` is `AdicCompletion.map_surjective` and needs no hypothesis at all.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AdicCompletion.PowerTorsionQuotient.solution` : Stacks 0BNG, as stated in
  `AdicCompletion.map_shortExact_and_isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot`.

## Implementation notes

The source deduces the lemma from [Stacks, Tag 03CA], the exactness of the inverse limit of a
tower of short exact sequences with surjective transition maps, applied to
`0 → M/(M ∩ IⁿN) → N/IⁿN → Q → 0`. The cofinality of `{IⁿM}` and `{M ∩ IⁿN}` is what identifies
`lim M/(M ∩ IⁿN)` with `M^∧`, and it is proved here as
`pow_add_smul_top_le_map_pow_smul_top`, used throughout in the pointwise form
`mem_pow_smul_top_of_apply_mem`. Given that containment the two conjuncts are short direct
arguments on Mathlib's `AdicCompletion`, which is a submodule of `∀ n, N ⧸ IⁿN` rather than a
categorical limit, so 03CA is not invoked: reindexing a Cauchy sequence by `n ↦ n + c` replaces the
passage between the two towers, and left exactness of `lim` is replaced by the two statements
`mk_eq_zero_of_forall_add_mem` and `map_mk_eq_mk_of_apply_eq_add`, which say that a shift of the
index by a constant changes neither vanishing nor the represented class in the completion.

Four of the helpers are general facts about adic completion with no counterpart in the Stacks Project.
`isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot`: a module annihilated by a power of `I` is `I`-adically
complete; Mathlib has this only for `I = ⊥` (`IsAdicComplete.bot`) and for subsingletons
(`IsAdicComplete.of_subsingleton`). `map_mk_eq_zero_iff`: the class of a Cauchy sequence `a` dies
under `AdicCompletion.map I f` exactly when `f (a n) ∈ IⁿN` for every `n`, which is what turns
membership in the kernel of a completed map into a condition on the filtration. And the two shift
lemmas named above, which are the cofinality of the filtrations `n ↦ Iⁿ` and `n ↦ Iⁿ⁺ᵏ` in the
only two forms this proof needs.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BNG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BNG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03CA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03CA)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.AdicCompletion.PowerTorsionQuotient

open _root_.AdicCompletion

section

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {I : Ideal R}
  {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
  {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]
  {Q : Type*} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q]

/-! ### Cauchy sequences, their images and shifts of the index

These three facts hold for every ideal and every linear map; they are the interface through which
the filtration conditions below are read off from, and turned into, statements in the completion.
-/

/-- A Cauchy sequence dies in `N^∧` under `AdicCompletion.map I f` exactly when `f (a n)` lies in
`Iⁿ • N` for every `n`. -/
private theorem map_mk_eq_zero_iff (f : M →ₗ[R] N) (a : AdicCauchySequence I M) :
    map I f (mk I M a) = 0 ↔ ∀ n, f (a n) ∈ (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R N) := by
  simp [AdicCompletion.ext_iff, map_mk, mk_apply_coe, Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero]

/-- Shifting the index by a constant `k` is cofinal, so a Cauchy sequence that lies in `Iⁿ • M`
after the shift already represents `0` in `M^∧`. -/
private theorem mk_eq_zero_of_forall_add_mem (k : ℕ) (a : AdicCauchySequence I M)
    (h : ∀ n, a (n + k) ∈ (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R M)) : mk I M a = 0 :=
  mk_zero_of I M a ⟨0, fun n _ ↦ ⟨n + k, Nat.le_add_right n k, n, le_rfl, h n⟩⟩

/-- Shifting the index by a constant `k` does not change the represented class, so a linear map
carrying `a` to `b` shifted by `k` carries the class of `a` to the class of `b`. -/
private theorem map_mk_eq_mk_of_apply_eq_add (f : M →ₗ[R] N) {k : ℕ}
    {a : AdicCauchySequence I M} {b : AdicCauchySequence I N} (h : ∀ n, f (a n) = b (n + k)) :
    map I f (mk I M a) = mk I N b := by
  refine AdicCompletion.ext fun n ↦ ?_
  simp only [map_val_apply, mk_apply_coe, Submodule.mkQ_apply, LinearMap.reduceModIdeal_apply,
    h n]
  exact AdicCauchySequence.mk_eq_mk (Nat.le_add_right n k) b

/-! ### A quotient annihilated by a power of the ideal -/

/-- If `I ^ c` annihilates `Q` then so does `I ^ n` for every `n ≥ c`. -/
private theorem eq_zero_of_mem_pow_smul_top {c n : ℕ} (hcn : c ≤ n)
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) {x : Q} (hx : x ∈ (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R Q)) :
    x = 0 :=
  (Submodule.mem_bot R).mp <|
    (Submodule.smul_mono_left (Ideal.pow_le_pow_right hcn)).trans hQ.le hx

/-- A module annihilated by a power of `I` is `I`-adically complete: the filtration `I ^ n • ⊤` is
`⊥` from index `c` on, so it has zero intersection and every `I`-adic Cauchy sequence is constant
from index `c` on. -/
private theorem isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot {c : ℕ}
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) : IsAdicComplete I Q where
  haus' _ hx := eq_zero_of_mem_pow_smul_top le_rfl hQ (SModEq.zero.mp (hx c))
  prec' q hq := ⟨q c, fun n ↦ (le_total n c).elim hq fun hcn ↦ by
    rw [sub_eq_zero.mp (eq_zero_of_mem_pow_smul_top le_rfl hQ (SModEq.sub_mem.mp (hq hcn)))]⟩

/-! ### Cofinality of `{IⁿM}` and `{M ∩ IⁿN}` -/

/-- If `0 → M → N → Q` is exact and `I ^ c` annihilates `Q`, then `I ^ c • N` is contained in the
image of `M`, since it maps into `I ^ c • Q = ⊥`. -/
private theorem pow_smul_top_le_range {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q} (hfg : Function.Exact f g)
    {c : ℕ} (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) :
    (I ^ c • ⊤ : Submodule R N) ≤ LinearMap.range f := by
  rw [← hfg.linearMap_ker_eq, LinearMap.le_ker_iff_map, Submodule.map_smul'']
  exact le_bot_iff.mp ((smul_mono_right _ le_top).trans hQ.le)

/-- Cofinality of the filtrations `{IⁿM}` and `{M ∩ IⁿN}`: with `I ^ c • Q = ⊥`, the submodule
`I ^ (n + c) • N` is contained in the image of `I ^ n • M`. -/
private theorem pow_add_smul_top_le_map_pow_smul_top {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q}
    (hfg : Function.Exact f g) {c : ℕ} (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) (n : ℕ) :
    (I ^ (n + c) • ⊤ : Submodule R N) ≤ Submodule.map f (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R M) :=
  calc (I ^ (n + c) • ⊤ : Submodule R N) = I ^ n • (I ^ c • ⊤ : Submodule R N) := by
        rw [pow_add, Submodule.mul_smul]
    _ ≤ I ^ n • LinearMap.range f := smul_mono_right _ (pow_smul_top_le_range hfg hQ)
    _ = Submodule.map f (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R M) := by
        rw [Submodule.map_smul'', Submodule.map_top]

/-- The form in which cofinality is used, and the one statement the main result rests on: for
injective `f`, membership of `f x` in `I ^ (n + c) • N` forces `x ∈ I ^ n • M`. -/
private theorem mem_pow_smul_top_of_apply_mem {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q}
    (hf : Function.Injective f) (hfg : Function.Exact f g) {c : ℕ}
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) {n : ℕ} {x : M}
    (hx : f x ∈ (I ^ (n + c) • ⊤ : Submodule R N)) : x ∈ (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R M) := by
  exact (Submodule.comap_map_eq_of_injective hf _).le
    (pow_add_smul_top_le_map_pow_smul_top hfg hQ n hx)

/-! ### The two halves of the completed sequence -/

/-- Adic completion preserves injectivity of `f` as soon as the cokernel is annihilated by a power
of `I`, with no Noetherian or finiteness hypothesis. -/
private theorem map_injective_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q}
    (hf : Function.Injective f) (hfg : Function.Exact f g) {c : ℕ}
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) : Function.Injective (map I f) := by
  rw [← LinearMap.ker_eq_bot, LinearMap.ker_eq_bot']
  intro x hx
  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective I M x
  exact mk_eq_zero_of_forall_add_mem c a fun n ↦
    mem_pow_smul_top_of_apply_mem hf hfg hQ ((map_mk_eq_zero_iff f a).mp hx (n + c))

/-- Exactness on representatives: a Cauchy sequence `b` in `N` whose class dies in `Q^∧` becomes,
after the shift `n ↦ n + c`, the image under `f` of a Cauchy sequence in `M`, and the shift leaves
the class of `b` in `N^∧` untouched. -/
private theorem exists_map_mk_eq_mk {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q}
    (hf : Function.Injective f) (hfg : Function.Exact f g) {c : ℕ}
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) {b : AdicCauchySequence I N}
    (hb : map I g (mk I N b) = 0) :
    ∃ a : AdicCauchySequence I M, map I f (mk I M a) = mk I N b := by
  have hrange (n : ℕ) : ∃ z : M, f z = b (n + c) := by
    rw [← LinearMap.mem_range, ← hfg.linearMap_ker_eq, LinearMap.mem_ker]
    exact eq_zero_of_mem_pow_smul_top (Nat.le_add_left c n) hQ
      ((map_mk_eq_zero_iff g b).mp hb (n + c))
  choose a ha using hrange
  have hcauchy : IsAdicCauchy I M a := by
    intro p q hpq
    rw [SModEq.sub_mem]
    refine mem_pow_smul_top_of_apply_mem hf hfg hQ (n := p) ?_
    rw [map_sub, ha p, ha q, ← SModEq.sub_mem]
    exact b.property (Nat.add_le_add_right hpq c)
  exact ⟨⟨a, hcauchy⟩, map_mk_eq_mk_of_apply_eq_add f ha⟩

/-- Adic completion is exact on `0 → M → N → Q → 0` as soon as `Q` is annihilated by a power of
`I`, with no Noetherian or finiteness hypothesis. -/
private theorem map_exact_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q}
    (hf : Function.Injective f) (hfg : Function.Exact f g) {c : ℕ}
    (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) : Function.Exact (map I f) (map I g) := by
  refine LinearMap.exact_of_comp_eq_zero_of_ker_le_range ?_ (fun y ↦ ?_)
  · rw [AdicCompletion.map_comp, hfg.linearMap_comp_eq_zero, AdicCompletion.map_zero]
  · intro hb
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective I N y
    obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := exists_map_mk_eq_mk hf hfg hQ (LinearMap.mem_ker.mp hb)
    exact LinearMap.mem_range.mpr ⟨mk I M a, ha⟩

end

/-- Completion of a short exact sequence `0 → M → N → Q → 0` with `I ^ c • Q = 0` is again short
exact, and `Q` is `I`-adically complete. -/
public theorem solution
    {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] {Q : Type*} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q]
    {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q} (hf : Function.Injective f)
    (hfg : Function.Exact f g) (hg : Function.Surjective g)
    {c : ℕ} (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) :
    Function.Injective (map I f) ∧ Function.Exact (map I f) (map I g) ∧
      Function.Surjective (map I g) ∧ IsAdicComplete I Q :=
  ⟨map_injective_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot hf hfg hQ,
    map_exact_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot hf hfg hQ,
    AdicCompletion.map_surjective I hg,
    isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot hQ⟩

end Stacks.AdicCompletion.PowerTorsionQuotient

end

/-!
# Completion of an extension whose quotient is killed by a power of the ideal

Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let

`0 → M → N → Q → 0`

be a short exact sequence of `R`-modules in which `Q` is annihilated by a power of `I`, say
`I ^ c • Q = 0`. Then `I`-adic completion turns it into a short exact sequence

`0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q → 0`,

whose third term is `Q` itself: for `n ≥ c` the quotient `Q ⧸ IⁿQ` is `Q`, so the tower defining
`Q^∧` is eventually constant and `Q → Q^∧` is bijective.

Adic completion is right exact always, so the content is the left half of the sequence, and the
mechanism is that the hypothesis on `Q` makes the two filtrations `{IⁿM}` and `{M ∩ IⁿN}` of `M`
cofinal in each other. Indeed `IᶜN ⊆ M`, hence `IⁿM ⊆ M ∩ IⁿN ⊆ Iⁿ⁻ᶜM` for `n ≥ c`, so
`M^∧ = lim M ⧸ (M ∩ IⁿN)`; the tower of short exact sequences
`0 → M ⧸ (M ∩ IⁿN) → N ⧸ IⁿN → Q → 0` has surjective transition maps, so its inverse limit is
short exact [Stacks, Tag 03CA]. Neither `R` Noetherian nor any finiteness of the modules is used;
that is what separates this lemma from `AdicCompletion.map_injective` and
`AdicCompletion.map_exact`, whose Noetherian-plus-finite hypotheses it trades for the hypothesis
on `Q`.

Two consequences are what the source goes on to use. Taking `M = N = R` and `f` multiplication by
a nonzerodivisor `f ∈ R` shows `f` is a nonzerodivisor on `R^∧` [Stacks, Tag 0BNS]. And since the
canonical maps `of` are natural, a complete `M` together with the bijectivity of `Q → Q^∧` forces
`N → N^∧` to be bijective: an extension of an `I`-power-torsion module by a complete module is
complete.

## Main results

* `AdicCompletion.map_shortExact_and_isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot`: for a short exact
  sequence `0 → M → N → Q → 0` of `R`-modules with `I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥`, the
  completed sequence `0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q^∧ → 0` is short exact, and `Q` is `I`-adically complete.

## Implementation notes

The source writes the completed sequence as `0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q → 0`, silently identifying `Q^∧`
with `Q`. Here `Q^∧` stands in the third spot, so that both maps of the sequence are the
functorial `AdicCompletion.map I f` and `AdicCompletion.map I g` and nothing has to be chosen; the
identification the source performs is then recorded as the conjunct `IsAdicComplete I Q`, which by
`AdicCompletion.of_bijective_iff` says exactly that the canonical `AdicCompletion.of I Q` is
bijective. Composing with its inverse turns the three exactness conjuncts into the source's
sequence, so nothing is lost.

The annihilation hypothesis is written Mathlib's way, `I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥`, the same
spelling in which `IⁿM` appears throughout the `AdicCompletion` API, and with an explicit exponent
`c` rather than as `∃ c, …`: the two are equivalent and a user has the exponent in hand.

Surjectivity of `AdicCompletion.map I g` needs no hypothesis on `Q` at all
(`AdicCompletion.map_surjective`); it is a conjunct because the source's conclusion is the
exactness of a sequence *ending in `0`*, not only its left half.

The three modules live in independent universes, `R` is not assumed Noetherian and the modules are
not assumed finite, since the source's proof uses none of that.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is idle: `hf`, `hfg` and `hg` are precisely the exactness of
`0 → M → N → Q → 0` that the conclusion completes, each used by one conjunct, and `hQ` is what
makes the completed sequence exact on the left and its third term `Q` — without it
`AdicCompletion.map I f` need not be injective, adic completion being merely right exact.
(2) The scalars and base are the source's: one ring `R`, all three modules `R`-modules, `f` and `g`
`R`-linear, and all completions taken at the same ideal `I ⊆ R`, with no base change; Mathlib's
`AdicCompletion.map I f` is `R^∧`-linear rather than `R`-linear, but only its underlying function
is used here, and it is the completion of the `R`-linear `f`. (3) The canonical maps are pinned,
not merely asserted to exist: the completed maps are `AdicCompletion.map I f` and
`AdicCompletion.map I g`, and the identification of the third term with `Q` is bijectivity of the
canonical `AdicCompletion.of I Q`, in the form `IsAdicComplete I Q`. (4) The instantiation is not
degenerate: over `R = ℤ` with `I = (2)`, `M = N = ℤ`, `f` multiplication by `2`, `Q = ℤ ⧸ 2ℤ` and
`c = 1`, writing `A` for the `2`-adic integers `AdicCompletion (2 : Ideal ℤ) ℤ`, the conclusion is
that `0 → A → A → ℤ ⧸ 2ℤ → 0` with first map multiplication by `2` is short exact, so `2` is a
nonzerodivisor on `A`; the source uses the lemma this way in [Stacks, Tag 0BNS], and it applies
just as well when `R` is not Noetherian and `N` is not finite.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BNG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BNG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03CA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03CA) for the inverse limit of a
  tower of short exact sequences with surjective transition maps
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BNS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BNS) for the nonzerodivisor
  application

## Tags

adic completion, exact sequence, torsion
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace AdicCompletion

/-- **Stacks 0BNG.** Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `0 → M → N → Q → 0` be a
short exact sequence of `R`-modules such that `Q` is annihilated by the power `I ^ c` of `I`. Then
`I`-adic completion produces a short exact sequence `0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q^∧ → 0`, and `Q` is
`I`-adically complete, so that this third term is `Q` itself as in the source's
`0 → M^∧ → N^∧ → Q → 0`. -/
@[stacks 0BNG "The third term is written `Q^∧`, and the conjunct `IsAdicComplete I Q` identifies \
it with the source's `Q`. No Noetherian or finiteness hypothesis, unlike `map_injective` and \
`map_exact`."]
public theorem map_shortExact_and_isAdicComplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_bot
    {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] {Q : Type*} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q]
    {f : M →ₗ[R] N} {g : N →ₗ[R] Q} (hf : Function.Injective f)
    (hfg : Function.Exact f g) (hg : Function.Surjective g)
    {c : ℕ} (hQ : I ^ c • (⊤ : Submodule R Q) = ⊥) :
    Function.Injective (map I f) ∧ Function.Exact (map I f) (map I g) ∧
      Function.Surjective (map I g) ∧ IsAdicComplete I Q :=
  Stacks.AdicCompletion.PowerTorsionQuotient.solution (c := c) (N := N) (M := M) (R := R)
    I hf hfg hg hQ

end AdicCompletion

end
